# Research design

## Project overview

This case study documents an applied quantitative survey research workflow,
covering questionnaire development, pilot testing, data preparation, and
initial quantitative evaluation.

The purpose of the pilot was to evaluate the functioning of the questionnaire
before its use in the main study and to identify potential issues in the
measurement and data-collection process.

## Research objectives

The pilot analysis was designed to:

- assess the structure and usability of the questionnaire;
- evaluate item-level response patterns;
- examine the internal consistency of proposed questionnaire scales;
- compare responses between predefined participant groups;
- identify potential issues that could inform questionnaire refinement.

## Questionnaire structure

The questionnaire contained 34 items organised into three conceptual areas:

1. Personal Development
2. Language
3. Global Citizenship

The public portfolio version uses synthetic data while preserving the
analytical structure of the original pilot.

## Pilot analysis workflow

The quantitative analysis followed these stages:

1. Data preparation
2. Questionnaire harmonisation
3. Response coding
4. Descriptive analysis
5. Item-level group comparisons
6. Multiple-comparison adjustment
7. Internal consistency analysis
8. Item-level correlation analysis

## Analytical considerations

The pilot analysis was exploratory. Item-level group comparisons were used to
identify patterns for further investigation rather than as confirmatory tests.

Because multiple item-level comparisons were conducted, p-values were adjusted
using the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure to control the false discovery rate.

Internal consistency was evaluated using Cronbach's alpha together with
item-level diagnostics. Reliability was not treated as a standalone criterion
for deciding whether a scale was suitable.

## Data confidentiality

The original participant-level dataset and confidential research materials
are not included in this public portfolio.

The reproducible analysis uses synthetic data generated within the R Markdown
document. The synthetic dataset is designed to reproduce the structure of the
analysis without exposing participant information or confidential research
materials.